{#-
Extractors:
  - items: AnchorExtractor("<ITEMS>", "</ITEMS>")

Override example:
  {{ set_anchor_extractor("items", "<MY_TAG>", "</MY_TAG>") }}
-#}
{% if viewer -%}
<|SECTION:{{ character.name.upper() }}|>
{{ knowledge_index.sheet(scene, character, viewer, "description") }}

{{ knowledge_index.description(scene, character, viewer) }}
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
{% endif -%}
{% if scene_history -%}
<|SECTION:SCENE|>
{% for line in scene_history -%}
{{ line }}

{% endfor %}
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
{% endif -%}
<|SECTION:CANDIDATES|>
{% for item in candidates %}
{{ loop.index }}. {{ item|condensed }}
{% endfor %}
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
<|SECTION:TASK|>
{{ character.name }} speaks next. The candidates above were retrieved for that line. Story context: {{ scene.context }}

{% if viewer -%}
Rewrite each candidate as what {{ character.name }} knows of it, from their own point of view: what they witnessed, were told, or is common knowledge in this world. Leave out any part {{ character.name }} could only know by reading the story from the outside. Drop a candidate {{ character.name }} knows nothing of.
{% else -%}
Rewrite each candidate to the part that matters for {{ character.name }}'s next line, keeping its meaning.
{% endif %}
Drop candidates that add nothing to the next line. Order the rest by how useful each one is for writing the line: what it should react to, recall or build on. The most useful candidate comes first.

Answer inside the tags, one kept candidate per line as `<number>. <rewritten text>`, in that order. Answer <ITEMS>none</ITEMS> when none of the candidates should reach the line.

Example:
<ITEMS>
3. The third candidate, rewritten.
1. The first candidate, rewritten.
</ITEMS>
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
{{ set_prepared_response("<ITEMS>") }}
